Ported from the Ipopt branch of Bertrand Neveu's Ibex fork.

    repository  https://github.com/bneveu/ibex-lib
    branch      qibex
    commit      045ea97cbd053f2c148cc443439861cbffd8935d  (2024-03-07)
    files       optim/examples-ipopt/ibex_LoupFinder{,Default}IpoptB.{h,cpp}
                optim/examples-ipopt/optimizer04-ipopt.cpp
                  -> src/bin/ibexopt-ipopt.cpp (see its header)

Two deliberate differences from upstream:

  * MINLP is out. The upstream files carry integer variables through
    System::minlp and get_integer_variables(), which this tree does not have.
    Every use was a guard at the edge, not structure: integer_and_bound_check()
    already collapses to its own bound-check branch for continuous problems, so
    removing it keeps the branch that was there rather than amputating one.

  * The class is named LoupFinderIpopt, without the B. Upstream keeps both an
    older finder that shells out to AMPL and this one that links Ipopt; only the
    latter is ported, so the suffix distinguishes nothing here.

The guided-diving work (CellDivingGuided, the dg* strategies, localbox >= 4 and
the continuation counters) is deliberately NOT here. It is uncommitted work in
the author's tree, and his own log records it as measured and neutral across
four successive implementations.

Two bugs were fixed rather than carried over:

  * The finder is its own Ipopt::TNLP and hands a raw "this" to OptimizeTNLP().
    Ipopt wraps it in SmartPtrs, and the last one released would "delete this"
    on an object Ipopt does not own -- upstream's optimizer04-ipopt.cpp works
    around it by never deleting the finder ("error with this delete in case of
    ipoptxninhc4"). The constructor now takes one self-reference and the
    destructor drops it after the application is gone, so the object can be
    deleted normally.

  * Ipopt prints its license banner on stdout at the first solve, which
    print_level does not suppress. ibexopt-ml speaks a line-of-JSON protocol on
    stdout, so the finder now sets Ipopt's own "sb yes" option.

One trap when porting the strategy file (src/bin/ibexopt-ipopt.cpp): upstream's
NormalizedSystem and ExtendedSystem take an extra third argument, relaxineq,
which this tree does not have. Passing its "true" through unchanged silently
means "extended" to NormalizedSystem here -- a normalized system with n+1
variables, which overflows the X-Taylor loup finder's LP. Inequalities are
therefore *not* relaxed by eps here, and node counts differ from the fork's for
that reason.

Four additive hooks were needed in this tree, all documented in place:
LoupFinder::{bound_check,bound_check_i,is_inner0,goal_ub0} and the ipopttime /
ampltime counters; Optimizer::{set_loup,set_uplo,set_loup_point} and a public
compute_ymax().
